  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 AM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\n- Multiple chances of showers and storms tonight into Sunday\n\nAnother night, and another round of convection just to our south \nover Illinois and Indiana where they don't need any more damaging \nstorms or rainfall. Up our way, we remain under a somewhat humid air \nmass, with little to no rain chances for one more night. \n\nWe do see some minor changes in the upper air pattern that will \nbring the better shower/storm chances to Lower Michigan as early as \ntonight, and keeping the chances in until Sunday. We see upper \nheights build a bit as the long wave trough over the Western U.S. \nmoves east a little, and we see some short waves come out of the \nlong wave trough. \n\nThis change will allow a short wave/MCV over Western KS early this \nmorning to move toward the area late Friday night/Saturday. As the \nMCV approaches, the moisture advection with the low level jet will \nweaken as is typical at that time of the day. This will bring a few \nshowers and maybe a rumble of thunder in Saturday morning. \n\nWe see instability build through Saturday as the front moves north \nand the very warm and moist air mass moves back over the area, \ncoinciding with diurnal peak heating. We see the instability \ncontinue to build Saturday evening as the low level jet with the \nnext wave pushes right at the area. It looks like this will be the \nfavored time and into Sunday morning for the best chances of showers \nand storms.\n\nCAPEs are shown to climb above 2000 J/kg Sat evening, and maybe even \nup to 3,000. Synoptic scale shear may be on the lower side, which \nwould limit severe potential a bit. There may be some better \nmesoscale deep layer shear associated with the short waves/MCV. That \nis a bit uncertain at this time, but there is a potential of some \nstrong to severe storms Sat evening/night. There may be some \ntraining of storms also with somewhat of a persistent low level jet \ncausing some backbuilding.\n \n- Cooling off and drying out later Sunday through Tuesday\n\nThe low jet jet from Saturday night will push east by daybreak \nSunday morning, and the cold front will be poised to steadily move \nthrough the area as the upper flow pushes it. Any lingering rain and \nclouds early will slowly improve in the afternoon behind the cold \nfrontal passage.\n\nWe will see upper ridging build in behind the system on Sunday, and \nbring dry weather as the low level flow from the north advects drier \nweather overhead. Dew points will go down, and temperatures will not \nbe nearly as warm. \n\n- Stronger system possible Wednesday\n\nThe upper ridge will control the weather for Monday and Tuesday, but \nwe will have nearly a zonal upper jet over the northern half of the \ncountry. What this will do is allow additional short waves ride it \nright into the region. There has been a wave shown to bring rain \nchances in for late Wednesday and Thursday. The latest data \nindicates that this wave may be stronger with a stronger jet \nsupporting it as it traverses the country. The sfc low is shown to \nbe relatively stronger, and potentially more impactful.\n\nWe will see another break in the weather then for Thursday as the \nWednesday system clears all of the moisture out of the area.",
